# Implement Upload of Genotypes

## Tags

* assigned: fredm, zachs, acenteno
* priority: medium
* keywords: gn-uploader, uploader, data, data upload, genotypes
* status: open
* type: feature request, feature-request


## Description

To add new genotypes to Genenetwork, a user needs to liaise with, and provide files to @acenteno to get the new genotypes online. This feature aims to remove the bottleneck that creates, as @acenteno has to do a lot of data curation and verification, then generate new files before finally running some scripts to enter the data into Genenetwork.

After the genotypes are successfully uploaded, then a number of extra steps need to be taken, among them:

* Run some data precomputation scripts, e.g. QTLReaper, …
* Pass data to @zachs to generate Genenetwork-spefic genotype files (i.e. =genotype/*.geno=, =genotype/*.json=, =bimbam/*.txt=, etc)
* Pass generated files to @fredm for setup in production (automate this if possible)

This issue will track the overall design and implementation of the feature, linking to other issues as necessary.

### Input Data Formats

Currently, genotype data can be provided to us (i.e. @acenteno and @zachs) in the following formats:

* Character-separated values (CSV) format very close to the custom .geno format
* BIMBAM format
* VCF Format
* R/qtl2 format

There is a huge host of file formats that can hold genotype information — we cannot hope to implement parsing for all of them. Instead, we need to only consider one or two main formats, then convert everything else into th(at/ose) main format(s).


### Data Processing

The uploaded files will need to parsed from the input formats previously, and the data translated into the storage formats mentioned above.

Our order of implementation for the data parsing will be as follows:

To start with, we can look into building a parser for any and all CSV-compatible formats (e.g. R/qtl2, plain CSV files, Excel files, etc). We can then implement parsing of **.vcf.gz** files, and convert all other formats to that.

**TODO**: This implies some method of detecting the type of file, classifying it and figuring out how to parse it to extract the relevant data.


#### Data Ingestion

The ingestion phase thus becomes something like:

* 01. User uploads a file of unknown provenance.
* 02. Detect type of file.
* 03. If file is `.vcf.gz` go to step 07.
* 04. If file is CSV-compatible (plain CSV, BIMBAM, R/qtl2, etc) go to 08.
* 05. If file is neither of the above check whether it's a known format that can be converted to `.vcf.gz` go to step 09
* 06. If file fails all checks (steps 03 to 05), exit immediately with a lot of fussing.
* 07. Parse the `.vcf.gz` then go to step 10.
* 08. Parse the CSV-compatible file then go to step 10.
* 09. Convert the file to `.vcf.gz` then go to step 07.
* 10. Convert data to internal, intermediate representation.
* 11. Send intermediate representation into QC phase

To detect the file types we could make use of mime types and magic bytes rather than file extensions that could be easily changed.

Conversion from other formats, e.g. PLINK, BGEN, etc. into `.vcf.gz` could be done via subprocess using CLI tools like plink and/or bcftools (look them up).


